# Project documents and fixtures are compared byte for byte by the persistence tests in
# B-09, so their line endings have to be the same on every machine that checks out this
# repository. Without this, a Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true turns every newline into
# two bytes and a round-trip test that should pass fails for a reason that has nothing to do
# with the code.
*.json text eol=lf

# The wrappers in scripts/ are shell scripts run by bash. A carriage return at the end of a
# line is part of the command as far as bash is concerned, so a CRLF checkout turns every one
# of them into a not-found error. They are LF on every machine.
scripts/* text eol=lf

# The archived licence texts are copies of what each crate ships, and a distribution has to
# be able to reproduce them. Line-ending conversion would rewrite them on checkout, so they
# are stored and delivered as exact bytes. tools/archive_licenses.py --check compares them
# against the crate sources, and that comparison is only meaningful if nothing rewrites
# them in between.
Licenses/** -text

# The verification artifacts are written by the tests with LF endings, and CI compares the
# committed copy against what a run produces. A Windows checkout that converts them shows
# every artifact as modified when nothing about the build has changed, which buries a real
# difference in noise. They are LF everywhere.
verification/**/*.md text eol=lf
verification/**/*.py text eol=lf

# Named by extension rather than as verification/**, deliberately. A blanket rule there also
# claims the rendered PNGs, and declaring a PNG to be text tells git to convert line endings
# inside it - which is to say, to corrupt every artifact the owner looks at. That is not a
# hypothetical: the blanket form was written here first and CI caught it rewriting eighteen
# images. Anything binary added under verification/ needs no rule; the absence of one is what
# keeps it intact.
